# Shell scripts must keep LF endings in every working tree, on every platform.
#
# `core.autocrlf=true` is the Windows default and rewrites checkouts to CRLF.
# The blob stays LF, so CI is unaffected -- but the file an operator actually
# runs is the working copy, and a CRLF `#!/usr/bin/env bash` fails with
# "bad interpreter: no such file or directory" the moment it is run under WSL,
# a container, or any Linux host. `scripts/check_self_approval.sh` exists to be
# run by an operator after a deploy, so that failure would land exactly where
# the script is meant to help.
*.sh text eol=lf

# The client governance package is provenance, and its SHA256SUMS.txt is only
# meaningful if the bytes on disk are the bytes the client sent.
#
# `core.autocrlf=true` is the Windows default and rewrites LF to CRLF on
# checkout. These files are LF, so without this rule every checksum in
# SHA256SUMS.txt would fail in a Windows working tree -- and it would fail
# quietly, because nothing else in the repository reads them byte-for-byte.
# `-text` disables all end-of-line conversion in both directions. Diffs stay
# readable; only the rewriting stops.
#
# db/tests/test_client_package_is_byte_preserved.py verifies the checksums
# against the working tree, so this rule has a test rather than a promise.
fixtures/offline_fairness_training/client_package_2026-08-24/** -text

# Captured telemetry payloads are evidence: the byte counts quoted in
# docs/presentations/ are wc -c of these files, and a reader re-runs the
# sensitive-field search against them. core.autocrlf=true would rewrite them on
# checkout and every figure would be wrong -- which is exactly what happened on
# the first attempt (17,443 bytes on disk, 17,232 stored). Same rule, same
# reason as the client package above.
docs/evidence/**/*.bin -text
